  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Fundamental Frequency in Fourier Series",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the Fourier-series fundamental frequency $\\omega_0 = 2\\pi/T_0$ from a periodic signal's period and use it as the spacing between harmonic components.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Frequency in Fourier Series",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fundamental Frequency in Fourier Series",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A Fourier series is read on a frequency grid. The key quantity on that grid is the fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$, because every harmonic component sits at an integer multiple of that spacing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If a periodic signal repeats every $T_0$ seconds, then one full cycle corresponds to the same $2\\pi$ radians of phase each time. That is why the period tells you the spacing of the Fourier-series frequency grid.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the simplest base case first: if $T_0 = 1$ s, then $\\omega_0 = 2\\pi$ rad/s. The harmonic locations then appear at $0,\\omega_0,2\\omega_0,3\\omega_0,\\dots$, so the spacing between adjacent components is always the same fundamental step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_k = k\\omega_0,\\quad k \\in \\mathbb{Z}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the reusable procedure is simple: find $T_0$ first, convert it to $\\omega_0$, and then use $\\omega_0$ to read the spacing between the harmonic components in the Fourier-series description.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Frequency in Fourier Series",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Computing $\\omega$_0 from a Given Fundamental Period",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal has $T_0 = 2$ s. Compute its fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the fundamental period $T_0$. Once $T_0$ is known, the fundamental angular frequency comes from direct substitution into $\\omega_0 = 2\\pi/T_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T_0} = \\frac{2\\pi}{2\\text{ s}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now simplify the result. With $T_0 = 2$ s, the spacing is $\\pi$ rad/s, so the Fourier-series frequency grid advances in steps of $\\pi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\pi\\ \\text{rad/s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Frequency in Fourier Series",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Computing $\\omega$_0 After Identifying the Fundamental Period",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic waveform repeats on intervals of length $3$ s. Identify $T_0$ and compute $\\omega_0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the repeat description. A waveform that repeats every $3$ s has fundamental period $T_0 = 3$ s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_0 = 3\\text{ s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the rule $\\omega_0 = 2\\pi/T_0$ and substitute the identified period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T_0} = \\frac{2\\pi}{3\\text{ s}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the fundamental angular frequency is $\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ rad/s. That is the spacing used by the Fourier-series harmonic grid.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\ \\text{rad/s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Frequency in Fourier Series",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Using $\\omega$_0 as the Spacing Between Fourier Series Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $\\omega_0 = \\pi/3$ rad/s, list the first four nonnegative Fourier-series frequencies and show how the spacing works.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the given spacing $\\omega_0$. For Fourier series, the frequencies form an equally spaced grid at integer multiples of $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_k = k\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $\\omega_0 = \\pi/3$ rad/s. For the first four nonnegative grid points, use $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\{0,\\ \\omega_0,\\ 2\\omega_0,\\ 3\\omega_0\\} = \\left\\{0,\\ \\frac{\\pi}{3},\\ \\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\ \\pi\\right\\}\\ \\text{rad/s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So each adjacent harmonic is separated by $\\pi/3$ rad/s, which is the same spacing as $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_{k+1} - \\omega_k = \\omega_0 = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\ \\text{rad/s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Frequency in Fourier Series",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Comparing Frequency Spacing for Different Fundamental Periods",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Signal A has $T_0 = 2$ s and signal B has $T_0 = 4$ s. Compute each $\\omega_0$ and state which harmonic grid is more widely spaced.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the period. Fundamental frequency changes inversely with $T_0$, so the shorter period gives the wider spacing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For Signal A, substitute $T_0 = 2$ s into the rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0^{(A)} = \\frac{2\\pi}{2\\text{ s}} = \\pi\\ \\text{rad/s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For Signal B, substitute $T_0 = 4$ s into the same rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0^{(B)} = \\frac{2\\pi}{4\\text{ s}} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\ \\text{rad/s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Signal A has the larger spacing, and it is twice Signal B's spacing. That is the inverse period-spacing relationship.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0^{(A)} = 2\\omega_0^{(B)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
